ICE roles, peer reflexive candidates and a wall clock retransmission
schedule for libpeer (extern/libpeer).

extern/libpeer is a git SUBMODULE, so its sources must not be edited in
place: the next `git submodule update` would throw the changes away.  This
patch is applied to the checked out tree by
tools/apply-libpeer-switch-patches.sh, right before libpeer is built (it is
the fifth patch of that series, after 0001-switch-webrtc-fixes,
0002-no-stdout-log, 0003-h265-depacketizer and 0004-dtls-diagnostics).

Why
---
Three independent defects kept the ICE state machine in "checking" forever,
and a fourth one made the retransmission rhythm follow the render frame rate.

1) The peer's USE-CANDIDATE was thrown away.

   `stun.c` parsed ICE-CONTROLLING / ICE-CONTROLLED / USE-CANDIDATE and then
   did nothing with them ("Do nothing").  We are the CONTROLLED agent -- the
   cloud's offer carries no `a=ice-lite`, so `peer_connection.c` puts us in
   AGENT_MODE_CONTROLLED -- which means the nomination belongs to the peer.
   RFC 8445 section 7.2.5.1 says the controlled agent nominates the pair the
   USE-CANDIDATE request arrived on.  We did not: the nomination had NO
   effect at all.

   `stun.c` now saves the attributes (has_use_candidate, has_ice_controlling,
   has_ice_controlled, ice_tie_breaker) and `agent_process_stun_request()`
   nominates the matching pair.

2) `typ prflx` was a parse error.

   `ice.c` only knew host / srflx / relay and answered "Unknown candidate
   type" for a peer reflexive candidate -- even though `ice.h` has had
   ICE_CANDIDATE_TYPE_PRFLX in its enum all along.  The cloud builds a prflx
   candidate for us when it learns our NAT mapping from our own check, so the
   candidate the cloud nominates simply did not exist on our side.

3) A binding request from an unknown address created nothing.

   `agent_process_stun_request()` used the source address only to send the
   binding response back.  The cloud learns our public mapping from our own
   connectivity check and then sends ITS check to that address: that address
   is not in `remote_candidates`, so there was no pair to nominate.

   The agent now creates a peer reflexive remote candidate for such a source
   and pairs it with the local candidates (RFC 8445 section 7.2.5).

   ★ ORDER: the prflx candidate is created AND paired FIRST, and only then
     is the USE-CANDIDATE honoured -- without a pair there is nothing to
     nominate, so the two have to work together.

   The candidate is built the way libwebrtc builds it
   (P2PTransportChannel::MaybeAddPeerReflexiveCandidate):
     * type preference 110 for prflx (host 126 / prflx 110 / srflx 100 /
       relay 0),
     * local preference 65535 (the old code used the ephemeral port),
     * component 1,
     * and the PRIORITY of the candidate is the PRIORITY the peer put into
       ITS request -- the peer computed it for the pair it is checking.  The
       request's PRIORITY attribute is parsed now (stun.c used to drop it).

   The resulting priorities match the reference values of the official
   client exactly:
       host  0x7EFFFFFF   prflx 0x6EFFFFFF   srflx 0x64FFFFFF   relay 0x00FFFFFF

   ★ ORDER: the formula is computed first and the request's PRIORITY
     OVERWRITES it -- the request wins, the formula is only the fallback for
     a request without a PRIORITY attribute.
   ★ A peer reflexive candidate is NEVER advertised: it is only ever a
     REMOTE candidate, and ice_candidate_to_description() does not
     serialize it (RFC 8445 section 15.1.1).

4) A binding response was accepted from anybody.

   `agent_process_stun_response()` counted every response it could parse and
   marked the nominated pair SUCCEEDED.  The response is now checked against
   the candidate the check was sent to (source address) and against the
   transaction id of a check that is still in flight; a response that does
   not match is dropped and counted in `rejected_binding_responses`.
   `addr_equal()` was a stub that always returned 1 ("// TODO") -- it now
   compares family, address and port.

   All THREE checks of libwebrtc's StunRequestManager::CheckResponse() are
   in place:
     1) MESSAGE-INTEGRITY + FINGERPRINT -- `stun_msg_is_valid()` verifies
        both (FINGERPRINT = CRC32, MESSAGE-INTEGRITY = HMAC-SHA1 over the
        message with the ICE short term credential, i.e. the peer's
        "a=ice-pwd"), so the remote password IS kept and the HMAC IS
        computed -- the check was already there, it was just the only one;
     2) the source address;
     3) the transaction id.

   ★ A dropped response does NOT trigger an immediate retry: the
     retransmission is driven by the wall clock schedule
     (agent_conncheck_due()), so bad responses cannot amplify the traffic.

5) The connectivity check was retransmitted on a FRAME count.

   `agent_connectivity_check()` sent a check when
   `nominated_pair->conncheck % AGENT_CONNCHECK_PERIOD == 0`, and
   `conncheck` counts the peer_connection_loop() calls -- and
   peer_connection_loop() is driven by the RENDER FRAME.  The ICE rhythm
   therefore followed the frame rate (23..31 fps on the device) instead of
   the clock: the second check went out after 100 frames (~3.3 s at 30 fps)
   and the give-up after 1000 frames (~47.6 s at 21 fps).  That is the
   "sometimes 3 seconds, sometimes 48 seconds" behaviour.

   The schedule is now a wall clock deadline: 100 ms, doubling on every
   unanswered check, capped at 2 s (RFC 8445 section 7.2.5).  `conncheck`
   stays as the "how many did we send" counter.

6) A pair that had succeeded did not end the ICE.

   `agent_select_candidate_pair()` set `selected_pair` in its SUCCEEDED
   branch but left `nominated_pair` alone -- and `agent_connectivity_check()`
   only ever looks at `nominated_pair`.  A binding response that arrived
   outside of a connectivity check call therefore left the peer connection in
   "checking" forever.  `nominated_pair` and `selected_pair` are kept in sync
   now, and a SUCCEEDED pair wins over a FROZEN one.

7) The CHECKING state had no timeout at all.

   `peer_connection.c` broke out of the loop when the candidate pair table
   was empty and never came back.  The state now fails after
   PEER_ICE_CHECKING_TIMEOUT_MS (5 s) with a reason
   (no_candidate_pairs / pair_never_nominated / no_binding_response /
   response_not_selected) and the pair table is logged WHILE we are still
   checking -- the diagnostic used to be printed only from dtls_start and
   transport_completed, so an ICE that never succeeded printed nothing.

8) One datagram per rendered frame.

   The engine read exactly ONE datagram per tick, which capped the session at
   ~60 packets per second (less at 23..31 fps) and dropped everything above
   it in the kernel -- that is the media loss.  Both the CHECKING and the
   COMPLETED state now DRAIN the socket (up to AGENT_RECV_DRAIN_MAX = 32
   datagrams per tick).

9) Two smaller ones.

   `AGENT_STUN_RECV_MAXTIMES` 1000 -> 100 (the synchronous STUN receive in
   createAnswer now waits 100 ms per server instead of 1 s), and the
   `SO_RCVBUF` request 2 MiB -> 256 KiB (2 MiB ALWAYS failed on the Switch,
   so the log never showed the real buffer size).

10) The DTLS certificate is an ECDSA P-256 key now.

   `CONFIG_DTLS_USE_ECDSA` was 0, so the self signed certificate was a
   1024 bit RSA key.  The official native client (StarRailCloud 4.6.0) is
   BoringSSL with a self signed ECDSA P-256 certificate, and every modern
   TLS stack refuses a 1024 bit RSA certificate by default (OpenSSL 3 with
   SECLEVEL >= 2).  libpeer already had the ECDSA branch and it generates
   exactly SECP256R1, so this is only the switch.  The certificate buffer is
   sized explicitly now (CONFIG_DTLS_CERT_BUFFER_SIZE) instead of after
   RSA_KEY_LENGTH, and the key generation failures are no longer ignored.

11) The first connectivity check left BEFORE the answer was on the wire.

   `peer_connection_create_answer()` entered the CHECKING state right away,
   so the first check went out one frame later -- while the answer was still
   on its way to the cloud.  The cloud had not processed our answer yet, so
   it did not know our ICE credentials and DROPPED the check.  The next check
   went out 100 frames later (~3.3 s at 30 fps) and THAT one was answered.
   That is the "sometimes it connects after 3 seconds" number -- it was not
   luck, it was the retransmission.

   The CHECKING transition now waits for the answer to be on the wire:
   `peer_connection_set_answer_sent()` releases it (the engine calls it when
   the page reports that the answer went out over the signalling channel),
   and a fallback (PEER_ANSWER_SENT_FALLBACK_MS) releases it when the report
   never arrives, so a page that does not implement the report still
   connects.

   The official client does the same: createAnswer() -> setLocalDescription()
   -> send the answer -> only then the ICE gathering starts.  Our libpeer
   entered CHECKING inside createAnswer(), i.e. before setLocalDescription().

The engine side changes of patch 0037 live in the engine patch itself.
